24. One distinction your supervisor may challenge you on

They may ask:

“You’re calling EBSE internal uncertainty. Is it really internal?”

The precise answer should be:

No, not in the sense of directly reading hidden activations.

We are estimating predictive uncertainty from repeated stochastic samples of the model’s answer distribution.

EBSE tells us how semantically dispersed the model’s possible answers are.

So I would avoid:

“EBSE measures the model’s true internal uncertainty.”

Instead say:

“EBSE provides an empirical estimate of predictive semantic uncertainty from the model’s answer distribution.”

That’s much safer.

⸻

25. Another likely question: Why not use accuracy − confidence?

Because that’s a different calibration question.

Classical calibration asks:

When the model says 80% confidence, is it correct approximately 80% of the time?

Our question is different.

We’re asking:

When predictive uncertainty changes across questions, does the uncertainty communicated by the explanation change correspondingly?

We’re studying uncertainty communication through explanations, not merely answer confidence calibration.

That’s what distinguishes the work.

⸻

26. Statistical terms you should know in one sentence each

If you’re asked quickly:

EBSE:
An entropy-based estimate of predictive uncertainty derived from semantic diversity across sampled model answers.

Spearman \rho:
Measures whether two variables increase or decrease together monotonically based on their ranks.

p-value:
Under the null hypothesis, the probability of obtaining a result at least as extreme as the observed one.

95% CI:
A range produced by a procedure that, over repeated samples, would contain the true parameter 95% of the time.

Bootstrap:
Repeatedly resampling the observed data with replacement to empirically estimate uncertainty in a statistic.

Mann–Whitney U:
A non-parametric test asking whether values in one independent group tend to rank higher than values in another.

Rank-biserial correlation:
An effect size describing the direction and magnitude of rank separation between two groups.

Δρ:
The difference between two Spearman correlations—in our case Joint minus Post-hoc.

Paired bootstrap:
Bootstrap where matched Joint/Post-hoc observations for the same questions are resampled together.

Wilcoxon signed-rank:
A non-parametric test of whether paired differences systematically lie above or below zero.

Statistical significance:
Evidence that an observed effect is difficult to explain under the specified null hypothesis; it does not tell us that the effect is large or practically important.

Effect size:
How large the observed relationship or difference actually is.

